# 0009. Three-pane layout: threads, conversation, context

- Status: Proposed
- Date: 2026-11-24

The user must see the conversation and each participant's state at once. This ADR fixes
the screen layout.

## Technical details
```
┌ threads ─┬ conversation ──────────────────────────────┬ context ────────┐
│ #auth    │ you    › fix the login bug                 │ claude  ? 41k ↑ │
│ #review  │ claude › plan…                             │ codex   ? 12k ↑ │
│          │   ↳ claude → codex  [handoff ▸]            │ llama ██░ ~38%  │
│          │ claude › applying fix  [tool: edit ▸]      │ (disabled)      │
├──────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────┤
│ > @codex review this                                    talk:on 2/6 turns│
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```
- Center: the chat, Claude Code look: speaker label, Markdown body, tool calls and
  handoffs as collapsed one-liners that expand on `Enter`. Each participant has a stable
  color; the user is neutral.
- Left (toggle `Ctrl-B`): threads from the log, newest first.
- Right (toggle `Ctrl-K`): per participant: state (idle, streaming, unavailable,
  disabled), role, and two separate numbers:
  - context fill: a bar only when it is knowable. For chat-only models roundtable builds
    the request itself, so it shows `~NN%` (estimate). For CLI agents it shows `?`,
    because their own compaction hides what they keep;
  - tokens sent and received this thread (`41k ↑`), cumulative, from the log.
- Bottom: input with completion popup; status line with talk switch, turns and budget used.
- Narrow terminals (< 100 cols) hide side panes by default; the chat never shrinks below
  60 cols.
- Colors follow the terminal palette (16 ANSI colors), so light and dark themes both work;
  no hard-coded RGB.

Consequences: everything the product promises is visible without a command. Cost: three
panes are cramped on small terminals, handled by the toggles.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: ratatui layout, Paragraph, List, Gauge
- Alternatives: chat only, info via commands: cleaner, but hides exactly what the tool is for; tmux split per agent (claude-squad style): shows agents, not their conversation
